---

0. OBJECTIVE

Transform GRID into a navigable “place-like” structured state space:

readable as geography (TRON)

writable as graph state (JARVIS)

stored as event-sourced memory (GitHub + Supabase)



---

1. CORE ARCHITECTURE

JARVIS → controls state changes (authority)
GRID   → stores structured world state (reality layer)
TRON   → renders GRID as navigable place (interface)


---

2. GRID PLACE MODEL

GRID is no longer abstract graph-only.

It becomes a spatial-semantic hybrid system.

2.1 PRIMARY ENTITIES

Entity	Definition

NODE	atomic unit of state (memory, system, file, concept)
EDGE	relationship between nodes (causal, temporal, structural)
REGION	clustered subgraph (district equivalent)
PORTAL	cross-region transition (link to other systems/repos)
ANCHOR	stable reference node (never restructured without HALO approval)
SHADOW	deprecated/hidden node (soft-archived state)



---

2.2 PLACE ABSTRACTION LAYER

GRID is interpreted as a “world map”:

Place Concept	GRID Equivalent

world	full graph
continent	major subsystem (emulator, memory, UI, agents)
region	clustered domain
city	active working system
building	module or file cluster
room	function or unit logic
corridor	dependency chain
landmark	stable node (high trust memory)
fog	unresolved / low-confidence state
void	deprecated or deleted node reference



---

3. TRON NAVIGATION LAYER

TRON does NOT modify GRID.

It provides semantic traversal UI logic.

3.1 FUNCTIONS

map rendering (GRID → spatial view)

path highlighting (ODIN-recommended routes)

anomaly highlighting (NEMESIS signals)

memory surfacing (MNEMOS retrieval overlays)

drift warnings (HUGINN alerts)



---

3.2 NAVIGATION MODES

Mode	Function

EXPLORE	free traversal of GRID
ROUTE	ODIN-driven optimal path
DEBUG	HALO + NEMESIS integrity view
REPLAY	LOKI-based historical traversal
BUILD	JARVIS-controlled mutation view



---

4. GRID STRUCTURAL LAYERS (EXPANDED)

GRID becomes multi-layered cognition space:

L0: Spatial Graph (nodes + edges)
L1: Causal Graph (why relationships exist)
L2: Field State (pressure, entropy, confidence)
L3: Vector Flow (direction of system evolution)
L4: Hypergraph (multi-node dependencies)
L5: Temporal Graph (history + versioning)


